<h1>About OpenFarm</h1>
<p class="lead"><strong>{{ site.short }}</strong></p>
<p>{{ site.tagline }}</p>
<p>OpenFarm is built in the spirit of the original <a href="https://github.com/openfarmcc">OpenFarm</a> (openfarm.cc), the free and open
  database of crowd-sourced growing guides started by Rory Aronson.</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>Pick a place and OpenFarm looks up its climate in your browser – no account, no tracking. Under the hood it uses the
  Köppen-Geiger classification (Beck et al. 2018, 0.5° grid), which describes the <em>whole</em> climate – summer heat,
  dry seasons, season length – not just winter cold like USDA zones. The {{ koppen.classes.length - 1 }} Köppen classes are
  grouped into {{ db.regions.length }} <a href="{{ '/regions/' | url }}">climate regions</a>, so every place on Earth gets one.
  Where a crop has no guide for your region yet, you see the guide from the most similar region, clearly labelled.</p>
{% include "how-it-works.njk" %}

<h2>Contribute</h2>
<p>Anyone can suggest improvements and every change is tracked.
  If you can edit a text file, you can contribute – use the “Edit this …” button at the bottom of any page.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Find your climate region and the crop you know how to grow.</li>
  <li>Copy <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/content/TEMPLATE.md"><code>content/TEMPLATE.md</code></a> to <code>content/guides/&lt;crop&gt;/&lt;region&gt;.md</code>.</li>
  <li>Fill in the calendar for <em>your</em> climate and at least one real source (extension service, horticultural society, research).</li>
  <li>Open a pull request. CI checks every file against the <a href="{{ '/schema/guide.schema.json' | url }}">JSON Schemas</a>.</li>
</ol>

<h2>The data model</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Crop</strong> (<code>content/crops/&lt;crop&gt;.md</code>, URL <code>/crops/&lt;crop&gt;/</code>) – the centre of the model: facts that are true everywhere (type, family, sun, size, seed-saving, perennial facts such as chill hours and rootstocks).</li>
  <li><strong>Variety groups and cultivars</strong> (<code>content/varieties/&lt;crop&gt;/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>) – horticultural groups and named cultivars. Some cultivar pages are names from Wikidata (CC0) only, with no growing instructions. Groups and curated varieties keep sourced traits.</li>
  <li><strong>Guide</strong> (file <code>content/guides/&lt;crop&gt;/&lt;region&gt;.md</code>, URL <code>/crops/&lt;crop&gt;/&lt;region&gt;/</code>) – how to grow one crop in one climate region: month calendar, spacing, suggested varieties, local problems. Old <code>/guides/…</code> URLs redirect here.</li>
  <li><strong>Climate regions</strong> – 15 regions grouping Köppen–Geiger classes (Beck et al. 2018). A place looks up its class in the browser and maps to one region.</li>
  <li><strong>Countries</strong> (<code>/countries/&lt;iso2&gt;/</code>) and <strong>cities / places</strong> (<code>/places/&lt;slug&gt;/</code>, or <code>?place=</code> on the home map) – climate share, maps, and “grows well here” lists derived only from sourced guides.</li>
  <li><strong>Hardiness zones</strong> – derived for a place from Open-Meteo ERA5 1991–2020 and labelled with official USDA 5 °F bounds; not an official USDA map.</li>
  <li><strong>Shared files</strong> – <a href="{{ '/problems/' | url }}">pests &amp; diseases</a>, <a href="{{ '/tools/' | url }}">tools</a>, companion pairs (with evidence level), rotation notes, and <a href="{{ '/crops/' | url }}#seed-sources">seed sources</a> are written once and reused when a source supports them.</li>
  <li><strong>Sources and API</strong> – every record names its sources and licence. Machine-readable JSON is published for each crop, region, country, place and zone, plus a dump at <a href="{{ '/api/all.json' | url }}">/api/all.json</a>. See the <a href="{{ '/api/' | url }}">API reference</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="sources">Sources &amp; licences</h2>
<p>Content is <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> (imported CC0 records stay CC0). Code is MIT.
  We only <em>copy</em> data from public domain, CC0, CC BY or CC BY-SA sources, with attribution.
  University and RHS pages are usually cited, not copied: we summarise in our own words and link.
  Wiki-derived rescue data is a scaffold, not an authority — it stays <a href="{{ '/help/#unreviewed' | url }}">Unreviewed</a> until someone checks it against a published source.
  Edible-part fields, when present, come from a cited authority and are for cultivated crops, not for identifying wild plants or foraging.
  Page credits come from git history (“Edited by”); the Sources list is published references only.
  The full policy, allowlist and exclusions are in <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/SOURCES.md">SOURCES.md</a>.</p>

<h2 id="roadmap">Roadmap</h2>
<ul class="roadmap">
  <li>Fill growing guides for climate regions that still have few or none, from credible regional sources, without inventing values.</li>
  <li>Review imported wiki-derived crop fields (spacing, sun, sowing, prose) against published sources and promote records to <code>origin: curated</code>.</li>
  <li>A richer rotation and neighbours view, as those features land.</li>
  <li>Upgrade climate data to Beck et al. (2023) 1991–2020 at 0.1° (also CC BY 4.0), and add native southern-hemisphere calendars.</li>
  <li>Translations, frost-date and day-length hints, and a climate-change view using later Köppen maps.</li>
  <li><strong>Offline ZIM for Kiwix.</strong> For major tagged releases, a release-triggered GitHub Action will build an offline OpenFarm ZIM: relative links, small bundled Commons images with attribution, Kiwix search instead of Pagefind, and graceful offline behaviour for location, calendar and profile. The action will run zimwriterfs and zimcheck, then attach the <code>.zim</code> to the GitHub release. A later step is to submit it to the Kiwix library.</li>
</ul>

